# vehje agent instructions

## Identity

Vehje is the embeddable, multi-input, multi-output IR framework. It is
not a language. A grammar plugs in on the input side and lowers surface
syntax into a shared IR; a target plugs in on the output side, declares
what it supports, and emits. Vehje owns the shared substrate, the Core
passes, the output machinery, and the runtime ABI, not any one language.
The rich general-purpose scripting language is one first-party consumer,
named later, on equal footing with the other consumers. Anything that
names a specific game, or a specific surface syntax, belongs in a
consumer repo, not here.

## Two sides: Rust compile side, Zig runtime side

**Rust compile side + Zig runtime side, joined by a C ABI defined and
validated in Rust.**

- **Compile side** (Rust): lower a program through the consumer's grammar
 to IR, resolve and check over the shared Core and families, run the
 family and effect inclusion checks, reduce every build-environment
 effect away, and emit a `Checked` residual. Constant evaluation and
 macro expansion happen here (they are build-environment reductions).
 Runs at dev time. Ships as a single static binary per target platform.
- **Runtime side** (Zig): execute the residual against the runtime
 environment. Small, fast, embeddable. Speaks C ABI. Pulls no `std`,
 no Rust runtime. The residual crosses tier-tagged: a flat serialized
 IR arena (baseline), an optimized bytecode, or native code (the ideal
 endgame, where the runtime is env-plumbing around LLVM/cranelift
 output). The tier is orthogonal to correctness; the effect proof is
 discharged in Rust before any lowering.
- **The compile-time / runtime split follows the effect axis**: what is
 parametrized over the build environment is the compiler's, what is
 parametrized over the runtime environment is the runtime's. The runtime
 never implements build-environment semantics, because the effect proof
 guarantees no such construct survives into the residual.

## Crates

The framework crate surface, settled by the extension-point round:

- `vehje-ir`. The shared representation: Core substrate forms, the
 `Family` extension trait plus effect classification, the family-set and
 effect-set typestate (reusing `hilavitkutin-api` `AccessSet`), the node
 builder, interner integration.
- `vehje-codegen`. The output machinery: the one generic fold, the
 `Target` trait (`Supports` family set, `Permits` effect set), the
 inclusion and effect checks, `Checked` gating, compile-time target
 composition (no `dyn`).
- `vehje-schedule`. The pass DAG over `arvo-graph`.
- `vehje-resolve`. The Core-level resolve pass, generic over the family
 set, with family-extension hooks.
- `vehje-check`. The Core-level check pass, generic over the family set,
 with family-extension hooks.
- `vehje-runtime-abi`. The tier-tagged C ABI and residual serialization.
- `vehje-runtime-driver`. Compiler-side dispatch over the ABI and the
 Zig runtime embedding.
- `vehje`. The two public contracts, orchestration, and the
 distribution-composition entry point.

Offered opt-in, not shipped now: generic front-end scaffolding, extracted
once a live consumer proves the shared shape. The framework owns no
front-end and no family.

## Foundations

This repo consumes two sibling foundation repos:

- [`arvo`](https://github.com/orgrinrt/arvo). numeric primitives +
 graph / sparse / bitmask / spectral / comb algorithms. Used for the
 pass DAG (`arvo-graph`) and artifact read/write sets
 (`arvo-bitmask`).
- [`hilavitkutin`](https://github.com/orgrinrt/hilavitkutin).
 Pipeline execution engine. Used by the **runtime** for morsel-driven
 macro / scratch / generative execution. The compiler itself does
 single-shot DAG walks and rarely needs the frame runtime.

Reuse arvo and hilavitkutin. Do not reimplement DAG topology, bitmask
ops, sparse storage, or pipeline scheduling in Vehje.

## Extension pattern

Consumers ship as **separate repos**. Each brings a grammar (a front-end
producing IR), a family (node kinds plus a `Family` impl plus effect
classification), any family-specific semantic checks, and one or more
targets (a `Target` impl declaring its `Supports` and `Permits` plus its
lowering). The known census: the first-party general-purpose language
(named later), `vehje-jomini` (Clausewitz across five Paradox titles),
`vehje-w3`, `vehje-ts4`, `vehje-lua`, the native target, polka, ikiuni's
scripting language, viola's lint-authoring language, and mockspace's
procedural-documents language.

Do not inline any consumer's surface syntax, family, or target into this
repo, and do not inline game-specific vocabulary. It makes the framework
non-generic and defeats the point.

## First-party language heritage (not a framework oracle)

A Python prototype of the eventual first-party general-purpose language
lives at `~/Dev/stellar-heritage/tools/vehje/` (~15k lines, 1500+ tests).
It is **heritage for that one consumer**, consulted when the first-party
language consumer is designed, not an oracle for the framework. The
framework's design authority is this round's locked topics and the design
docs. When the first-party language consumer is built, read the Python for
that language's semantics; never translate it line by line, and never
treat it as authority over the framework's own shape.

## Compiler posture

Strict by default, across both the Vehje language and the Rust
compiler implementation:

- Deny warnings at crate roots: `unused`, `unreachable_code`,
 `unused_must_use`, `unused_imports`, `dead_code`.
- No `.unwrap()` / `.expect()` outside tests and documented infallible
 paths.
- Tagged enum + trait dispatch; no `dyn Trait` / `TypeId` / `std::any`
 in framework code. Compile-time dispatch wins.
- Descriptive error variants. Errors carry context (span, phase,
 expected/actual), not just a string.
- Procedural macros only for Vehje language semantics. `macro_rules!`
 is acceptable internally in the Rust compiler implementation for
 boilerplate reduction, but never for Vehje itself. macros in the
 language are proc-only, scheduler-pass-driven, and typechecked.

## Single-binary distribution

End users should not need Python, Deno,.NET, JVM, or any runtime
alongside Vehje. `cargo build --release` produces one static binary
per target. No dynamic runtime deps. No plugin shared-object loading
at the compiler layer (the runtime may embed differently).

## Commit style

See `skills/commit-style/`. Short version: `type: lowercase imperative
message`, no parens, no trailing period, under 72 characters.

## What not to do

- Do not edit `.claude/` or `.github/` output. Those are generated
 from `mock/agent/*.tmpl` by `cargo mock`. Edit the template.
- Do not inline any consumer's surface syntax, family, or target, or
 game-specific vocabulary (`clausewitz`, `witcher`, `stellaris`,
 `paradox`, …) into framework code. Use a consumer repo.
- Do not reimplement graph / DAG / bitmask / sparse / pipeline / set-
 typestate primitives. Use `arvo` and `hilavitkutin` (the `AccessSet`
 typestate is reused, not reinvented).
- Do not treat the Python prototype as a framework oracle. It is
 heritage for the first-party language consumer, read when that
 consumer is built, never translated line by line.
- Do not bypass the design-round state machine by renaming changelist
 files manually. Use `cargo mock lock / unlock / deprecate / close`.
- Do not reach for `dyn Trait` / `TypeId` / `std::any` in framework
 code; dispatch is compile-time composition. The Zig runtime is exempt
 and may load dynamically.
